{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DyvjoJES4z93xHffQgJv9L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for a Dream simulation of their EMNLP 2026 submission on structural self-consistency for IE. Let me analyze the data carefully.\n\nKey findings from the experiments:\n1. Structural consistency (voting_conf, SJ) >> logprob for quality estimation on NER\n2. Correlation-selection gap: no signal beats greedy on NER best-of-N; only logprob helps on RE\n3. Task-complexity boundary: RE degrades due to compound error propagation\n\nThe paper has pivoted from \"TTS improvement\" to \"confidence estimation + correlation-selection gap.\"\n\n\nNow I'm looking at the experimental coverage across models and datasets—I've got the 2×2 matrix completed with both Qwen and LLaMA on SciERC and CoNLL, tested at N=8 and N=16, and validated robustness across 3 seeds. The signal comparison framework covers 5 different approaches, though WNUT was dropped due to negative results. Across these settings, structural signals like voting confidence and SJ consistently outperform logprob for NER, with correlations ranging from 0.36-0.50 versus logprob's 0.14-0.32, and voting-based AUROC hovering around 0.70-0.73.\n\nFor relation extraction, the picture is murkier—SJ shows weak correlations around 0.21-0.30 while voting confidence performs better at 0.38-0.42, and logprob's AUROC spans a wide range from 0.56-0.76. The best-of-N selection experiments reveal that no signal beats greedy for NER, but logprob gains a meaningful 2.4 percentage points over greedy for RE. I'm thinking through how this lands with reviewers at EMNLP 2026—the best case is all three contributions resonate cleanly and the paper gets accepted to Main, but more realistically there's pushback on novelty or some claims face skepticism.\n\nThe worst scenario is reviewers dismiss this as a negative result repackaged, questioning whether structural signals matter when logprob already works for selection, or they see the scope as too narrow. I'm identifying the key assumptions that make or break acceptance: whether structural consistency actually outperforms logprob for quality estimation, whether the correlation-selection gap reads as novel insight rather than just a negative finding, whether the 2×2 generalization matrix holds across models and datasets, whether the task-complexity diagnostic adds real value, and whether I can position this clearly against concurrent work like Nédellec & Xia 2026.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the specific metrics that will determine how reviewers react—voting confidence and SJ AUROC scores, the gap between structural and logprob approaches, how consistently structural signals win across different configurations, the F1 difference in selection tasks, and bootstrap confidence intervals. Since the data's already collected, the tiers really depend on how well the paper frames these results and whether the presentation quality matches venue standards.\n\nThe key question is whether the cross-dataset generalization holds up, if the shift from TTS to confidence estimation resonates with reviewers, and whether the novelty stands against related concurrent work. I should anchor the acceptance tiers around metrics like the structural-versus-logprob AUROC gap, the win rate across different settings, and reviewer scores on novelty and overall quality. Let me think about what the user is actually asking for—they want three concrete scenarios for how this paper might be received, each with specific assumptions about what reviewers will accept and corresponding metric thresholds. Since the experiments are already done, I'm really defining what different reviewer perceptions would look like.\n\nFor the best case, I'm assuming the quality estimation framing lands as a genuine contribution, the correlation-selection gap reads as a novel insight rather than a limitation, and the experimental scope (2×2 matrix with 3 seeds) passes the generalization bar—that would put us at reviewer scores around 3.5-4.0 with acceptance to the main conference. For the median case, the quality estimation work gets accepted but maybe seen as incremental, so the paper's fate hinges more on how much weight reviewers give to the selection failure finding.\n\nNow I need to structure this as YAML with the ARR review criteria in mind, translating these narrative scenarios into specific score ranges and decision outcomes. Looking at the cross-config comparisons, the structural approach consistently outperforms logprob across all tested configurations—NER tasks on both SciERC and CoNLL datasets show structural ρ values ranging from 0.36 to 0.45 versus logprob's 0.14 to 0.32, and the pattern holds for relation extraction as well.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to frame the contribution tiers based on what reviewers might prioritize: whether they see quality estimation for structured IE as a standalone contribution or if they want to see selection improvements too, how they interpret the correlation-selection gap, whether cross-setting consistency feels sufficient, and whether concurrent work poses a novelty threat. I'll structure the tiers around reviewer perception of these key dimensions.\n\nWriting out the tier definitions in YAML... Now I'm looking at the probability ranges and performance metrics across different configurations. The structural approach consistently outperforms logprob-based quality estimation across NER tasks with an AUROC gap around 7-17%, and reviewers seem to grasp the correlation-selection gap as a key insight. The three-claim framing remains solid, with the structural advantage for quality estimation as the main contribution.\n\nBut there's skepticism to address—reviewers are questioning whether the quality estimation finding is truly novel, and they want stronger evidence of generalization across more datasets or larger models before accepting the correlation-selection gap as a standalone contribution. The work on related papers like Nédellec & Xia 2026 suggests partial overlap, and some negative results in the RE domain are raising concerns about scope. what'\" — the gap itself is clear but the practical implications remain fuzzy. I need to reframe the narrative: soften the novelty claims by positioning the five-signal taxonomy as a methodological contribution rather than novel discovery, make Claim 2 actionable with concrete guidance on signal selection and decision logic, and recast Claim 3 from a diagnostic framework to an empirical observation. Adding deeper ablation studies could help offset the perceived novelty gap, and I should target the Findings track instead of Main. The worst-case scenario is reviewers dismissing this as a negative-result paper showing self-consistency doesn't work for IE, with insufficient depth to justify publication and concurrent work potentially scooping the contribution. as doing the same study; our structural metrics seen as minor extension\"\n      - \"Correlation-selection gap dismissed as 'obvious — instance-level ≠ sample-level is known'\"\n      - \"Only 2 NER datasets / 1 RE dataset / only 8B models viewed as fatally narrow\"\n      - \"WNUT failure (abandoned) discovered or inferred, further weakening generalization\"\n      - \"Reviewers expect selection improvement or reject; quality estimation alone seen as insufficient venue contribution\"\n    number_ranges:\n      reviewer_overall: [2.5, 3.0]\n      acceptance_prob_main: [0.02, 0.10]\n      acceptance_prob_findings: [0.20, 0.40]\n      perceived_novelty: [2.0, 2.5]\n      perceived_soundness: [2.5, 3.5]\n      cross_config_structural_gt_logprob_rho_win_rate: [0.70, 0.85]\n      ner_auroc_gap_structural_minus_logprob: [0.07, 0.17]\n      correlation_selection_gap_clarity: \"low — dismissed as known or obvious\"\n    narrative_direction: The real path forward is to completely reframe the paper around the correlation-selection gap as the central finding rather than a secondary observation. Instead of positioning this as a comparison study with a positive result, I should lean into the surprising negative result—why self-consistency fails for structured prediction—and market it as a cautionary analysis within the test-time scaling literature. The quality estimation data becomes supporting evidence explaining why the gap exists, grounded in how instance-level aggregation destroys per-sample ranking information. This shifts the contribution toward theoretical implications for scaling laws and treats the negative results themselves as the insight, aiming for EMNLP but with a fundamentally different paper archetype.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the consistency constraint holds across all metrics—that best ≥ median ≥ worst. Looking through the ranges, most check out, but I notice the cross-config win rate shows [0.85, 1.0] ≥ [0.70, 0.85] ≥ [0.70, 0.85], where median and worst are identical. That actually makes sense because this particular metric is fixed data rather than a range estimate.\n\nThis is making me reconsider the structure—I should distinguish between the fixed empirical metrics that don't change across tiers and the reviewer perception metrics that do. The empirical data itself is constant; what varies is how reviewers interpret and perceive it. So I should reorganize to use only perception-based metrics for the number_ranges, while separately noting what aspects of the fixed data each tier emphasizes. Though I'm also wondering if the user's request for \"key experimental metric expected ranges\" means I should account for scenarios where the paper writing process might expose issues in the underlying data.\n\nLooking at the actual registry, exp_017 with LLaMA on CoNLL is already complete with an SJ_rho of 0.431, matching the Qwen results, so that data is already accounted for. I think the right framing is that the tiers represent how